{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452718-uintah-basin-ghost-story","id":1452718,"title":"Uintah Basin ghost story","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-17T17:14:09-07:00","id":1452718,"title":"Uintah Basin ghost story","content":"I have this idea for a short chronicle that I've been thinking about running for our teenager and some of her friends.<br /> They're all from Utah, so I thought it would be cool to introduce them to some of the stuff in their own backyard.<br /> <br /> Below is a rough story outline. General comments and suggestions welcome, as well as more specific discussion about the subject matter (see: the other thread on cultural appropriation in gaming). Trying to keep the game short, rules-lite and creepy without being too overtly scary (player ages are in the 12-16 range).<br /> <br /> The story starts with a few friends driving home from a camping trip in the Uintah Basin. It was a perfect trip, full of s'mores and swimming and hiking. One of them forgot their hat at the campsite, but she doesn't remember until they're hours away. Oh well. <br /> <br /> It's night. Late summer. One of them just got her license. They're coming through the canyon when a deer runs out into the road in front of them.<br /> They get out to inspect the damage. The deer lies very still on the ground. But it's missing most of it's hair. It's skin is the mottled yellow-purple of an old bruise, and there are these bulbous growths all over it. <br /> <br /> They hear a noise coming from around the bend. Footsteps or...maybe drums? Then: sudden movement and the sound of something moving through the underbrush. When they look back, the deer is gone. <br /> <br /> They return home, but it almost seems like something came home with them. Strange things keep happening.<br /> A phone goes missing and turns up in the microwave. It's history shows seventeen outgoing calls to a number that's not in service.<br /> Their dog is suddenly nowhere to be found, but they can hear him barking. It sounds like it's coming from inside the walls? It's almost midnight when they find him, shaking and whining, in the closet.<br /> They wake up to something rapping on their window. Their apartment is on the third story.<br /> There is a huge barn owl outside at night. It always seems to be staring right at them. <br /> <br /> These occurances increase in frequency and severity, going from mysterious to dangerous, as they try to understand what's going on. They join online forums, talk to so-called &quot;experts&quot; of the paranormal, and learn of the strangeness in the Uintah Basin, and that the answers to their questions may lie on the Ute reservations. The term &quot;skinwalker&quot; comes up more than once. <br /> <br /> But the tribes are not especially open to talking about this sort of thing with outsiders. In their stories, what a skinwalker is and what it does will change depending on which story it is and who's telling it. This isn't some ancient piece of mythology they can research and catalog; this is a living piece of the Ute and Dine'\u00e9 culture, and it's one they mean to protect.<br /> But after they share some of their own experiences, they learn that, in some stories, skinwalkers can use an item or token of a person to...cast spells? Curse? Astrally project themselves? --from great distances. In such stories, if a skinwalker got ahold of something that belonged to you, you'd never be safe from them until you got it back.<br /> And if they really wanted to get to the bottom of things...well. They could go to Dark Canyon. But that is a bad place. Very few go there willingly, and those that do try not to linger.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dark Canyon lives up to it's name. They search the area for clues, and the sun begins to set. They find a cave at the base of the canyon. There are several sets of tracks in the area, though it's hard to tell what made them, what with the recent rains and the failing light and all.<br /> And then they hear it. Something making its way down the canyon. Something big. What breaks through the treeline is like something out of a nightmare. It must weigh at least five hundred pounds, with hairless, mottled skin the color of a deep bruise, a set of slavering jaws and wickedly curved 6-inch claws. It bellows and charges, and the girls must fight for their lives at the bottom of Dark Canyon as night falls around them. <br /> <br /> When it seems like there is no escape, when it seems like the skinwalker has them at last and there is nowhere left to run, a single shot rings out. The beast falls.<br /> There's a deputy from the San Juan County Sheriff's Department. He had received an anonymous call about a group of girls who might be in trouble down in the canyon. There had been several sightings of animals with mange in the area, and sick animals can be unpredictable, even aggressive. Like this bear, here. <br /> <br /> After making sure everyone is okay and all the appropriate paperwork is filled out, the girls turn back toward the road. Maybe it was all in their heads after all.<br /> Before they go, they notice something a little deeper inside the cave. It's their hat, lying on the ground. As they pick it up, it's almost like they can hear footsteps--or drums--somewhere deep within the caves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}